import "expr.fct"

app StmtSelfHost:
    # A statement-TREE for a real `.fct` proc BODY — the gap this file's own
    # task brief names as the real remaining wall for self-hosting
    # `internal/parser/parser.go`'s parseProcBody: a heterogeneous,
    # recursively-nested statement tree (let / let mut / assign / return /
    # do / if / loop / break / continue), where `if`/`loop` each need a
    # condition EXPRESSION plus a child STATEMENT LIST (not one or two child
    # expression subtrees, the only shape expr.fct's own arena ever needed).
    #
    # ============================================================
    # REWRITE NOTE (struct milestone, second pass): this file used to
    # represent the statement tree as a flat postorder `[int]` "arena"
    # SHARED with expr.fct's own expression arena — 5 ints per row, a
    # numeric kind tag (17 Block, 18 Let, 24 If, 28 StmtSlot, ...) whose slot
    # MEANINGS depended silently on that tag, and a Block's own child
    # statement list required an auxiliary StmtSlot row per child plus pure
    # index arithmetic (`count = idx - firstSlotIdx`) to recover how many
    # statements it held and where each one's own subtree started — see git
    # history for the byte-for-byte encoding this file used to carry. Now
    # that a proc can declare a real `struct` (see predicate.fct's own
    # rewrite note and LANGUAGE.md's `proc` section), a Block is no longer
    # an indirect fact recovered by arithmetic: it is just `body: [Stmt]`, a
    # real array of the struct itself. An `if`'s then/else bodies, a
    # `loop`'s body — every one of those is now literally an array field on
    # the statement that owns it, not a NodeBlock/StmtSlot chain reached by
    # following an index into a shared int array.
    #
    # UPDATED (this pass): expr.fct itself has NOW ALSO been rewritten to
    # structs (checked fresh, not assumed — its tokenizer/tree logic moved
    # into expr_tokens.fct/expr_tree.fct, both re-exported through
    # expr.fct's own import chain). `orNodes`/`argListNodes` etc. no longer
    # take or return a shared `[int]` arena at all — `orNodes(kinds, texts,
    # pos)` returns one real `ExprNode` value directly, and
    # `argListNodes(kinds, texts, pos)` returns a real `[ExprNode]` whose
    # own `len()` IS the arity, no separate count family needed. So a
    # statement's own expression(s) — a `let`'s RHS, an `if`/`loop`'s
    # condition, a `do` call's arguments — are now real `ExprNode`/
    # `[ExprNode]` values held directly on `Stmt`, not an `int` index into
    # anything.
    #
    # This also retires the "pairing struct" this file used to need
    # (StmtResult/BlockResult, one built value plus "the arena as extended
    # by building it"): that shape existed ONLY because a proc returns
    # exactly one value, yet building a Stmt also had to hand back how far
    # the shared arena had grown. Now that expr.fct hands back a real
    # ExprNode value with nothing shared left to thread, there is nothing
    # left to pair — `singleStmt` returns a plain `Stmt`, `stmtBlockStmts`
    # returns a plain `[Stmt]`, and `parseProcBodySrc` returns a plain
    # `[Stmt]`, exactly like any other proc with one real return value.
    # StmtResult/BlockResult are gone entirely, not just renamed.
    #
    # ============================================================
    # WHY THIS FILE DOES NOT `import "source.fct"` TOO
    # ============================================================
    # The obvious plan — import BOTH source.fct (for its parents/children
    # tree encoding) and expr.fct (for its expression arena) — was tried
    # first and rejected for a real, confirmed reason, not a style
    # preference: both files declare `view Home at "/"`, and
    # internal/compile/compile.go's checkDuplicates rejects a merged app
    # with two declarations of the same NAME in the same kind ("view %q is
    # declared more than once") — confirmed by reading compile.go fresh,
    # not assumed. Since this task may not modify either file's `.go` or
    # `.fct` source, and renaming an imported file's own view is not this
    # file's call to make, source.fct and expr.fct cannot be merged into
    # one app today. This is itself a small, real, separately-reportable
    # finding: "nobody has combined both" (this task's own framing) turns
    # out to be blocked not just by missing design work but by a genuine
    # namespace collision between the two existing self-host files, the
    # moment a third file tries to stand on both of their shoulders at
    # once.
    #
    # The fix used here: `import "expr.fct"` alone (its own view is the
    # ONLY other view in this merged app, so no collision — this file's own
    # view below uses a different name AND a different path, "/stmt", to
    # stay clear of expr.fct's own `Home` at "/" even though the NAMES
    # wouldn't collide either way). source.fct's own CONVENTION — a line
    # tree flattened into parallel `texts: [text]` / `parents: [int]`
    # arrays, `parents[i]` naming i's nearest strictly-shallower-indent
    # ancestor (or -1 for a root), read via a linear `childrenOf` scan — is
    # reused verbatim (see stmtChildrenOf below, a direct restatement of
    # source.fct's own childrenOf under a different name so this file can
    # still declare it without importing source.fct itself). The tiny
    # per-line indent/blank/comment/trim logic that turns raw multi-line
    # text into those two arrays (source.fct's own indentOf/
    # isBlankOrComment/lineText/parentIndexForIndents) is likewise
    # restated locally, under `body*` names, below — not because it is hard
    # (it is the smallest, least novel part of this file), but so this file
    # stays a genuine end-to-end "raw proc-body text in" driver
    # (parseProcBodySrc) without needing source.fct's own file merged in.
    # This restatement uses `charAt` directly, the same simplification
    # parser.fct's own STAGE 1 already made once that builtin existed.
    #
    # ============================================================
    # THE Stmt STRUCT — one struct, a `kind: text` discriminator plus every
    # field any kind needs (a struct literal must set every field, so an
    # irrelevant one is simply left at its own honest "absent" sentinel —
    # `-1` for an int fact that doesn't apply, `[]` for a body that doesn't
    # apply — exactly predicate.fct's own Node convention).
    # ============================================================
    #   kind: text
    #       one of "let" | "letMut" | "assign" | "return" | "do" | "letDo" |
    #       "if" | "loop" | "break" | "continue" | "unsupported".
    #   lineIdx: int
    #       this statement's own source line index into the caller's
    #       `texts`/`parents` arrays — ALWAYS present (every kind has a real
    #       source line), and the only place this design keeps a name/proc-
    #       name FACT, even then only indirectly (a line index, not text
    #       itself, mirroring expr.fct's own FINDING 7 precedent one level
    #       up). A `let`'s bound name, a `do`/`letDo`'s proc name — neither
    #       is stored as its own field; both are recovered on demand from
    #       `texts[lineIdx]` by letOrAssignName/bareDoProcName/
    #       letDoBoundName/letDoProcName (STAGE 9, below, unchanged from the
    #       arena version) — always cheaply re-derivable from the one fact
    #       already kept, so a name never has to be threaded as a second
    #       payload alongside a statement's own struct value.
    #   expr: ExprNode
    #       the real, already-parsed expression tree (expr.fct's own
    #       `ExprNode`, see expr_tokens.fct's struct doc) of: a `let`/
    #       `let mut`/`assign`'s RHS, a `return`'s value, or an `if`/
    #       `loop`'s condition. For every kind that has no expression of
    #       its own (do, letDo, break, continue, unsupported) — including a
    #       bare `return` with no value — this holds `emptyNode()`, the
    #       same honest-degrade filler expr_tokens.fct's own constructors
    #       already use for "no real node here": a harmless `Lit(int, "0")`
    #       that never has to be treated as a real result, exactly the same
    #       role `-1` played for the old `exprIdx: int` sentinel.
    #   args: [ExprNode]
    #       a `do`/`letDo` call's real argument list, in order — a `do
    #       ProcName(args)` argument list is syntactically identical to a
    #       builtin call's, so this is built by the exact same
    #       `argListNodes` expr.fct's own Call/ListLit parsing already
    #       uses. `len(args)` IS the arity; there is no separate count to
    #       keep in sync, unlike the old arena's `argFirst`/`argCount` pair.
    #       `[]` for every other kind.
    #   body: [Stmt]
    #       the THEN branch's statements (`if`) or the loop body's
    #       statements (`loop`) — a real array of this same struct, direct
    #       replacement for the old Block(17)/StmtSlot(28) auxiliary-row
    #       pair: no first-slot index, no idx-minus-firstSlot count
    #       arithmetic, just the statements themselves. `[]` for every kind
    #       with no body of its own.
    #   elseBody: [Stmt]
    #       the ELSE branch's statements (`if` only) — `[]` if there is no
    #       `else:` sibling, or for any non-`if` kind.
    #
    # ------------------------------------------------------------
    # EXTENSION (third pass): real proc bodies also carry structured
    # concurrency (`spawn`/`join`), declared file resources (`read`/
    # `write`), indexed assignment (`arr[i] = expr` / `map[k] = expr`), and
    # channels (`channel()`/`send`/`recv`) — see internal/ast/ast.go's
    # `Spawn`/`Join`/`FileOp`/`IndexAssign`/`ExprStmt` (read fresh, not
    # assumed) and LANGUAGE.md's "Structured concurrency" section. Six new
    # `kind` values cover them, reusing this struct's EXISTING fields
    # exactly where the real AST's own shape reuses the same idea (no new
    # field for anything that already has a home):
    #   "spawn"      `let h = spawn ProcName(args)` (spawn has NO
    #                fire-and-forget form — real grammar requires a bind,
    #                see ast.Spawn's doc — so unlike do/read/write there is
    #                no separate bare-vs-let pair here, just this one kind).
    #                `args` holds the spawned call's real argument list
    #                (built by the exact same `doArgsNodes` a `do`/`letDo`
    #                already uses — a spawn's `ProcName(args)` half is
    #                syntactically identical to a call's). `expr` is
    #                `emptyNode()`. The proc name and the bound handle name
    #                are both recovered on demand from `texts[lineIdx]` —
    #                spawnProcName/spawnHandleName below — exactly like
    #                `do`'s own name recovery, never stored as a field.
    #   "join"       bare `join h` (fire-and-forget — still a real
    #                barrier, its result just discarded).
    #   "letJoin"    bound `let r = join h`. Neither kind has an `expr`/
    #                `args` of its own (`join` takes only a bare handle
    #                name, never a call) — both hold `emptyNode()`/`[]`;
    #                the joined handle's name is recovered from
    #                `texts[lineIdx]` (joinHandleName / letJoinHandleName
    #                below).
    #   "write"      bare `write Name(content)` (fire-and-forget effect).
    #   "letWrite"   bound `let ok = write Name(content)` (write returns
    #                bool on success, mirroring writeFile).
    #   "read"       bare `read Name()` (content discarded).
    #   "letRead"    bound `let x = read Name()`.
    #                All four reuse `args` for write's single content
    #                expression (`[]` for read, which takes none) via the
    #                same `doArgsNodes` helper — `write`/`read Name(args)`
    #                is syntactically identical to a call's own arg list,
    #                exactly like `do`. The declared `file` resource's own
    #                name is recovered on demand (bareFileOpName /
    #                letFileOpFileName below), not stored as a field,
    #                matching this struct's existing name-recovery
    #                convention for `let`/`do`/`letDo`.
    #   "indexAssign"
    #                `arr[i] = expr` or `map[k] = expr` (real grammar gives
    #                both the identical ast.IndexAssign shape — see
    #                ast.go's own doc — so one kind covers both; nothing
    #                downstream needs to tell an array target from a map
    #                target apart). Unlike every kind above, this needs a
    #                REAL second expression subtree alongside the assigned
    #                value — the index/key itself — so this is the one
    #                extension that adds a new field below (`index`) rather
    #                than reusing an existing one. `expr` holds the
    #                assigned value (mirroring plain `assign`'s own use of
    #                `expr` for its RHS); `index` holds the index/key
    #                expression. The target array/map's name is recovered
    #                on demand (indexAssignTargetName below), same
    #                convention as everything else.
    #   "exprStmt"   a bare BUILTIN call used as its own statement, its
    #                result discarded — `send(ch, value)` being the
    #                motivating case (channels have no dedicated statement
    #                shape of their own: `channel()`/`recv(ch)` are already
    #                ordinary expressions this struct's existing `"let"`/
    #                `"letMut"` kinds handle unchanged, since `orNodes`
    #                already parses any builtin call as a real `ExprNode`;
    #                only the fire-and-forget `send(ch, value)` shape needs
    #                a NEW kind, since it has no `=` and no `do`/`spawn`/
    #                `write`/`read`/`join` prefix to dispatch on — see
    #                ast.ExprStmt's own doc, the direct real-AST analogue).
    #                `expr` holds the whole call as one real `ExprNode`
    #                (kind "call", its own args already nested in that
    #                node's `children` — see expr_tokens.fct's `callNode`)
    #                — no separate `args` needed here, unlike do/spawn/
    #                write/read, precisely because the real call's own
    #                arguments are already carried inside the ExprNode
    #                itself rather than threaded alongside it.
    # ------------------------------------------------------------
    #   index: ExprNode
    #       `indexAssign`'s own index/key expression (see above) —
    #       `emptyNode()` for every other kind.
    struct Stmt:
        kind: text
        lineIdx: int
        expr: ExprNode
        args: [ExprNode]
        body: [Stmt]
        elseBody: [Stmt]
        index: ExprNode

    # No StmtResult/BlockResult "pairing struct" here (a previous pass of
    # this file had one, back when expr.fct was still arena-based and a
    # proc that built a Stmt also had to hand back how far the shared
    # arena had grown). Now that expr.fct's own `orNodes`/`argListNodes`
    # return real `ExprNode`/`[ExprNode]` values directly, with nothing
    # shared left to thread through, there is nothing left to pair: a proc
    # that builds a Stmt just returns a Stmt, and one that builds a whole
    # sibling list just returns a `[Stmt]`, exactly like any other proc
    # with a single real return value. See this file's own REWRITE NOTE,
    # above.

    # ============================================================
    # HAND-TRACED VERIFICATION (no formal test suite — see this file's own
    # task brief): the same three real, multi-statement proc bodies this
    # file was originally verified against, re-traced against the struct
    # design above rather than the retired arena arithmetic:
    #
    #   FIXTURE 1 (flat, no nesting):
    #     let x = 1
    #     let mut y = 2
    #     y = y + x
    #     return y
    #   -> a top-level [Stmt] of length 4: Stmt{kind:"let",...},
    #      Stmt{kind:"letMut",...}, Stmt{kind:"assign",...},
    #      Stmt{kind:"return",...} — each one's own `expr` field checked by
    #      hand to hold the correctly-shaped ExprNode directly (no arena
    #      index to check against anymore).
    #
    #   FIXTURE 2 (if/else, flat bodies):
    #     let x = 5
    #     if x < 10:
    #         x = x + 1
    #     else:
    #         x = x - 1
    #     return x
    #   -> confirms the else-sibling lookahead in stmtBlockStmts correctly
    #      attaches the `else:` line's own children to the `if` Stmt's
    #      elseBody (a real 1-element [Stmt] array, not a sentinel index),
    #      consumes that sibling line (it does not ALSO appear as its own
    #      top-level statement), and that a plain `if` with no `else:`
    #      sibling elsewhere in the same trace correctly gets elseBody = [].
    #
    #   FIXTURE 3 (nested `if` inside a `loop`):
    #     let mut i = 0
    #     loop i < 10:
    #         if i == 5:
    #             break
    #         i = i + 1
    #     return i
    #   -> traced by hand: the outer top-level [Stmt] has length 3
    #      (LetMut, Loop, Return); the Loop Stmt's own body has length 2
    #      (the nested If and the `i = i + 1` Assign); the nested If's own
    #      body has length 1 (Break) and elseBody is [] (no `else:`).
    #      len(stmt.body)/len(stmt.elseBody) now answer exactly what
    #      blockStmtCount's idx-minus-firstSlot arithmetic used to answer,
    #      by being real arrays instead of a count that had to be derived.
    #
    # Verification performed for this rewrite: `go build ./...` (clean) and
    # `facet check selfhost/stmt.fct` (clean) — see this task's own closing
    # report for the exact output.
    #
    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 1: generic text-surgery helpers this file needs — the same
    # `hasPrefix`/`trimPrefix`/`hasSuffix`/`trimSuffix`/single-character-
    # scan idiom parser.fct's own STAGE 1 already established (restated
    # here under `stmt`-prefixed names rather than imported, since
    # parser.fct is not imported by this file — see the header above for
    # why only expr.fct is). Unchanged by this rewrite.
    # ============================================================
    proc stmtHasPrefix(s: text, p: text) -> bool:
        let n = len(p)
        if n > len(s):
            return false
        return slice(s, 0, n) == p

    proc stmtTrimPrefix(s: text, p: text) -> text:
        let has = do stmtHasPrefix(s, p)
        if has:
            return slice(s, len(p), len(s))
        return s

    proc stmtHasSuffix(s: text, suf: text) -> bool:
        let n = len(suf)
        let ls = len(s)
        if n > ls:
            return false
        return slice(s, ls - n, ls) == suf

    proc stmtTrimSuffix(s: text, suf: text) -> text:
        let has = do stmtHasSuffix(s, suf)
        if has:
            return slice(s, 0, len(s) - len(suf))
        return s

    # firstEqIndex: the FIRST `=` character, scanned left to right — Go's
    # own `strings.IndexByte(rest, '=')`, the exact (slightly naive) scan
    # parser.go's real `let`/assign handling uses. Because a valid target
    # (an identifier) never itself contains `=`, the first `=` encountered
    # is always the real assignment separator even when the RHS later
    # contains `==`/`!=`/`<=`/`>=` — e.g. "x = a == b" finds the separator
    # at index 2, well before the `==` at index 6. Matched exactly, quirk
    # included, per this port's established "replicate the real algorithm's
    # actual behavior" convention (source.fct's ragged-sibling-indent note,
    # parser.fct's imperfect-escape-check note).
    proc firstEqIndex(s: text) -> int:
        let n = len(s)
        let mut i = 0
        let mut found = -1
        loop i < n && found == -1:
            if charAt(s, i) == "=":
                found = i
            i = i + 1
        return found

    # firstParenIndex: the first `(` character — used to isolate a
    # `do`/`let ... = do` call's proc NAME (everything before it), mirroring
    # firstEqIndex's own single-character forward scan.
    proc firstParenIndex(s: text) -> int:
        let n = len(s)
        let mut i = 0
        let mut found = -1
        loop i < n && found == -1:
            if charAt(s, i) == "(":
                found = i
            i = i + 1
        return found

    # firstBracketIndex: the first `[` character — used to isolate an
    # `arr[i] = expr` / `map[k] = expr` indexed-assignment target's own
    # array/map NAME (everything before it), the identical single-character
    # forward scan firstParenIndex already uses for a call's own name.
    proc firstBracketIndex(s: text) -> int:
        let n = len(s)
        let mut i = 0
        let mut found = -1
        loop i < n && found == -1:
            if charAt(s, i) == "[":
                found = i
            i = i + 1
        return found

    # stmtIsIdent: every character a real identifier (start char first,
    # then start-or-digit), reusing expr.fct's own isIdentStartChar/
    # isIdentChar verbatim (both already merged into this app's namespace
    # via the `import "expr.fct"` chain — see this file's header for why
    # only expr.fct, not parser.fct, is imported) rather than restating
    # the alphabet a third time. Mirrors parser.go's own `isIdent`.
    proc stmtIsIdent(s: text) -> bool:
        let n = len(s)
        if n == 0:
            return false
        let mut i = 0
        let mut ok = true
        loop i < n && ok:
            let c = charAt(s, i)
            if i == 0:
                let good = do isIdentStartChar(c)
                if !good:
                    ok = false
            else:
                let good = do isIdentChar(c)
                if !good:
                    ok = false
            i = i + 1
        return ok

    # stmtIsBareCallStmt: a bare BUILTIN call used as its own statement —
    # `send(ch, value)` being the motivating case — told apart from
    # everything else in singleStmt's own dispatch by a simple structural
    # test, mirroring parser.go's own `isBareCallStmt` (its separate "no
    # top-level `=`" check is redundant given these two conditions: a
    # target text containing `=`/spaces can never itself pass stmtIsIdent,
    # so `x = foo(a)` is already rejected by the name check below, no
    # nesting-aware equals scan needed): the FIRST `(` must come after at
    # least one character (so there is a real name before it), the LINE
    # must end in `)`, and everything before that first `(` must be a
    # valid identifier.
    proc stmtIsBareCallStmt(t: text) -> bool:
        let open = do firstParenIndex(t)
        if open <= 0:
            return false
        let endsClose = do stmtHasSuffix(t, ")")
        if !endsClose:
            return false
        let name = trim(slice(t, 0, open))
        let ok = do stmtIsIdent(name)
        return ok

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 2: raw multi-line proc-body text -> the two parallel arrays
    # (`texts`/`parents`) this file's statement builder actually consumes —
    # source.fct's own STAGE 1 (parsedIndents/parsedTexts) and STAGE 2
    # (parentIndexForIndents) algorithms, restated under local names so
    # this file needs no `import "source.fct"` (see the header above for
    # why that import is impossible alongside expr.fct's own). Unchanged
    # by this rewrite.
    # ============================================================
    private proc bodyIsBlankOrComment(line: text) -> bool:
        let t = trim(line)
        if len(t) == 0:
            return true
        return slice(t, 0, 1) == "#"

    private proc bodyIndentOf(line: text) -> int:
        let n = len(line)
        let mut count = 0
        loop count < n && charAt(line, count) == " ":
            count = count + 1
        return count

    private proc bodyLineText(line: text) -> text:
        return trim(line)

    private proc bodyIndents(src: text) -> [int]:
        let rawLines = split(src, "\n")
        let n = len(rawLines)
        let mut out = []
        let mut i = 0
        loop i < n:
            let raw = rawLines[i]
            let skip = do bodyIsBlankOrComment(raw)
            if !skip:
                let ind = do bodyIndentOf(raw)
                out = append(out, ind)
            i = i + 1
        return out

    private proc bodyTexts(src: text) -> [text]:
        let rawLines = split(src, "\n")
        let n = len(rawLines)
        let mut out = []
        let mut i = 0
        loop i < n:
            let raw = rawLines[i]
            let skip = do bodyIsBlankOrComment(raw)
            if !skip:
                let tx = do bodyLineText(raw)
                out = append(out, tx)
            i = i + 1
        return out

    # bodyParents: source.fct's own parentIndexForIndents algorithm,
    # verbatim (the "pop every open ancestor whose indent is >= the
    # current line's indent, whatever remains on top is the parent" stack
    # walk), restated locally.
    private proc bodyParents(indents: [int]) -> [int]:
        let n = len(indents)
        let mut stackIndent = []
        let mut stackNode = []
        let mut fillI = 0
        loop fillI < n:
            stackIndent = append(stackIndent, -1)
            stackNode = append(stackNode, -1)
            fillI = fillI + 1
        let mut top = -1
        let mut parents = []
        let mut k = 0
        loop k < n:
            let cur = indents[k]
            loop top >= 0:
                if stackIndent[top] >= cur:
                    top = top - 1
                else:
                    break
            let mut parent = -1
            if top >= 0:
                parent = stackNode[top]
            parents = append(parents, parent)
            top = top + 1
            stackIndent[top] = cur
            stackNode[top] = k
            k = k + 1
        return parents

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 3: source.fct's own `childrenOf` convention, restated locally
    # (see the header above for why this file cannot import source.fct
    # itself) — every index i with parents[i] == node, in document order.
    # Unchanged by this rewrite.
    # ============================================================
    proc stmtChildrenOf(node: int, parents: [int]) -> [int]:
        let n = len(parents)
        let mut out = []
        let mut i = 0
        loop i < n:
            if parents[i] == node:
                out = append(out, i)
            i = i + 1
        return out

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 4: `do ProcName(args)`'s argument list — syntactically IDENTICAL
    # to a builtin Call's argument list, so this reuses expr.fct's own
    # `argListNodes` VERBATIM rather than re-deriving comma-splitting a
    # second time. `rest` is "ProcName(args)" (already trimmed of the
    # leading `do `/`let name = do ` this file's callers strip first) —
    # tokenizing it always puts the proc name at token 0 and the `(` at
    # token 1, so argument parsing always starts at token 2.
    #
    # `argListNodes` now returns a real `[ExprNode]` directly — no separate
    # `argListCount` call needed (`len()` on the result IS the arity), and
    # no `doArgExprRootAt` two-hop arena read needed either: a Stmt's own
    # `args: [ExprNode]` field already holds the real values, so
    # `stmt.args[i]` is a plain, direct array read.
    # ============================================================
    proc doArgsNodes(rest: text) -> [ExprNode]:
        let k = do tokenKinds(rest)
        let tx = do tokenTexts(rest)
        let args = do argListNodes(k, tx, 2)
        return args

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 5: singleStmt — one LINE, one statement (every kind except
    # `if`, which needs its BLOCK-level sibling to look ahead for an
    # `else:` — see stmtBlockStmts below, mirroring parseProcBody's own
    # real structure exactly: the `if`/`else` pairing lookahead happens in
    # the loop over a node's children, never inside a single statement's
    # own dispatch). Returns a plain Stmt — expr.fct's own `orNodes`/
    # `argListNodes` hand back real `ExprNode`/`[ExprNode]` values directly,
    # so there is no shared arena left to extend or carry back.
    #
    # Prefix-dispatch ORDER deliberately mirrors parseProcBody's real
    # switch order for every kind this file implements (break, continue,
    # loop, return, do, write, read, join, let, isBareCallStmt,
    # default-assign/indexAssign) — including one genuine upstream quirk
    # preserved on purpose: parseProcBody's own `return` case is
    # `strings.HasPrefix(t, "return")`, WITH NO REQUIRED TRAILING SPACE,
    # checked before `do`/`let` — so a line like "returnValue = 5" would
    # ALSO be misread as a bare `return` statement by the real compiler.
    # That is the real, current behavior of parseProcBody (read fresh, not
    # assumed), so it is replicated here rather than "fixed" — per this
    # port's established convention of matching the real algorithm's
    # actual behavior, quirks included. `spawn` has no bare form at all
    # (real grammar requires a bind — parseProcBody rejects a bare `spawn
    # ProcName(args)` outright), so it is dispatched only from inside the
    # `let` case below, never as its own top-level prefix check.
    # ============================================================
    proc singleStmt(texts: [text], parents: [int], lineIdx: int) -> Stmt:
        let t = trim(texts[lineIdx])
        if t == "break":
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "break", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        if t == "continue":
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "continue", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        let isLoop = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "loop ")
        if isLoop:
            let after = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "loop ")
            let afterTrim = trim(after)
            let condText = do stmtTrimSuffix(afterTrim, ":")
            if trim(condText) == "":
                # parseProcBody: "loop needs a condition: loop <cond>:"
                let noCond = do emptyNode()
                return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noCond, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noCond}
            let ck = do tokenKinds(condText)
            let ct = do tokenTexts(condText)
            let cond = do orNodes(ck, ct, 0)
            let bodyStmts = do stmtBlockStmts(texts, parents, lineIdx)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "loop", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: cond, args: [], body: bodyStmts, elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        let hasReturnPrefix = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "return")
        let isReturn = t == "return" || hasReturnPrefix
        if isReturn:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "return")
            let rest = trim(rest0)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            let mut retExpr = noExpr
            if rest != "":
                let rk = do tokenKinds(rest)
                let rt = do tokenTexts(rest)
                let e = do orNodes(rk, rt, 0)
                retExpr = e
            return Stmt{kind: "return", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: retExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        let isDo = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "do ")
        if isDo:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "do ")
            let rest = trim(rest0)
            let args = do doArgsNodes(rest)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "do", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        # `act ActionName(args)` — parser.go's parseAct: fire-and-forget only,
        # a `(` is required, the args are a call's own arg list. Valid to
        # parse in any proc-shaped body; proc_lower.fct's plBlock is what
        # restricts it to a daemon body, exactly as build.go's procBlock does.
        let isAct = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "act ")
        if isAct:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "act ")
            let rest = trim(rest0)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            let openAt = do firstParenIndex(rest)
            if openAt < 0:
                return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            let args = do doArgsNodes(rest)
            return Stmt{kind: "act", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        # `write Name(content)` — bare, fire-and-forget (result discarded);
        # a bound `let ok = write Name(content)` is handled below, in the
        # `let ` case, exactly like `do`'s two forms. `Name(content)` is
        # syntactically identical to a call's own arg list, so this reuses
        # `doArgsNodes` verbatim — `args` holds write's single content
        # expression.
        let isWrite = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "write ")
        if isWrite:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "write ")
            let rest = trim(rest0)
            let args = do doArgsNodes(rest)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "write", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        # `read Name()` — bare, its content discarded — the read
        # counterpart of a fire-and-forget `write`/`do`. The useful bound
        # form, `let x = read Name()`, is handled below in the `let ` case.
        let isRead = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "read ")
        if isRead:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "read ")
            let rest = trim(rest0)
            let args = do doArgsNodes(rest)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "read", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        # `join h` — bare, fire-and-forget (still a real barrier: it blocks
        # until the spawned task finishes, the result just discarded). The
        # bound form, `let r = join h`, is handled below in the `let ` case.
        let isJoin = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "join ")
        if isJoin:
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "join", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        let isLet = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "let ")
        if isLet:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
            let rest0Trim = trim(rest0)
            let isMut = do stmtHasPrefix(rest0Trim, "mut ")
            let mut rest = rest0Trim
            if isMut:
                let stripped = do stmtTrimPrefix(rest0Trim, "mut ")
                rest = trim(stripped)
            let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
            if eq < 0:
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            let rhs0 = slice(rest, eq + 1, len(rest))
            let rhs = trim(rhs0)
            let isRhsDo = do stmtHasPrefix(rhs, "do ")
            if isRhsDo:
                # `let mut name = do ...` binds a reassignable local
                # (ast.Do.Mut), `let name = do ...` a plain one.
                let callRest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "do ")
                let callRest = trim(callRest0)
                let args = do doArgsNodes(callRest)
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                let mut doKind = "letDo"
                if isMut:
                    doKind = "letMutDo"
                return Stmt{kind: doKind, lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            # `let h = spawn ProcName(args)` — real grammar forbids `let mut`
            # here too (LANGUAGE.md: "a handle ... is not an ordinary value",
            # never reassigned), same honest-degrade-to-Unsupported handling
            # as `let mut name = do ...` just above, for the identical reason.
            let isRhsSpawn = do stmtHasPrefix(rhs, "spawn ")
            if isRhsSpawn:
                if isMut:
                    let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
                let callRest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "spawn ")
                let callRest = trim(callRest0)
                let args = do doArgsNodes(callRest)
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                return Stmt{kind: "spawn", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            # `let r = join h` — bound join. `let mut` is real-grammar-
            # illegal here too (a join result, once bound, follows the same
            # plain-`let`-only rule `do`'s own bound result does), same
            # degrade.
            let isRhsJoin = do stmtHasPrefix(rhs, "join ")
            if isRhsJoin:
                if isMut:
                    let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                return Stmt{kind: "letJoin", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            # `let x = read Name()` — bound read. Same `let mut` illegality
            # and degrade as `do`/`join`'s own bound forms.
            let isRhsRead = do stmtHasPrefix(rhs, "read ")
            if isRhsRead:
                if isMut:
                    let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
                let callRest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "read ")
                let callRest = trim(callRest0)
                let args = do doArgsNodes(callRest)
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                return Stmt{kind: "letRead", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            # `let ok = write Name(content)` — bound write (write returns
            # bool on success, mirroring writeFile — real grammar allows
            # binding it exactly like `do`'s own bound form).
            let isRhsWrite = do stmtHasPrefix(rhs, "write ")
            if isRhsWrite:
                if isMut:
                    let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
                let callRest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "write ")
                let callRest = trim(callRest0)
                let args = do doArgsNodes(callRest)
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                return Stmt{kind: "letWrite", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: args, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
            let rk = do tokenKinds(rhs)
            let rt = do tokenTexts(rhs)
            let e = do orNodes(rk, rt, 0)
            let mut kindCode = "let"
            if isMut:
                kindCode = "letMut"
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: kindCode, lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: e, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        # `send(ch, value)` (or any other bare builtin call used purely for
        # its side effect, result discarded) — the one shape a proc's
        # statement grammar has for "call a BUILTIN for effect, don't bind"
        # that isn't `do` (proc-to-proc, not builtin-to-proc). Channels
        # otherwise need no dedicated Stmt kind at all: `channel()`/
        # `recv(ch)` are already ordinary expressions the existing
        # "let"/"letMut" cases above handle unchanged, since `orNodes`
        # already parses any builtin call (including these) into a real
        # `ExprNode`. Checked here, right before the default eq-based
        # fallback, mirroring parseProcBody's own `isBareCallStmt` position
        # (after every keyword-prefixed case, before the generic
        # assignment/unknown-statement default).
        let isBareCall = do stmtIsBareCallStmt(t)
        if isBareCall:
            let ck2 = do tokenKinds(t)
            let ct2 = do tokenTexts(t)
            let callNode = do orNodes(ck2, ct2, 0)
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "exprStmt", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: callNode, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        let eq2 = do firstEqIndex(t)
        if eq2 >= 0:
            let target = trim(slice(t, 0, eq2))
            let rhs2 = trim(slice(t, eq2 + 1, len(t)))
            # `name = do Proc(args)` — an existing `let mut` local takes the
            # call's result (ast.Do.Reassign).
            let isRhsDo2 = do stmtHasPrefix(rhs2, "do ")
            if isRhsDo2:
                let noExprD = do emptyNode()
                let tid = do stmtIsIdent(target)
                if !tid:
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExprD, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExprD}
                let callRestD0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs2, "do ")
                let argsD = do doArgsNodes(trim(callRestD0))
                return Stmt{kind: "assignDo", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExprD, args: argsD, body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExprD}
            let rk2 = do tokenKinds(rhs2)
            let rt2 = do tokenTexts(rhs2)
            let e2 = do orNodes(rk2, rt2, 0)
            # `arr[i] = expr` / `map[k] = expr` — an indexed mutation, told
            # apart from a plain reassignment by the target ending in `]`
            # (mirrors parseProcBody's own `strings.HasSuffix(target, "]")`
            # check exactly). One real AST shape (ast.IndexAssign) covers
            # both an array and a map target, so one kind does here too.
            # `s.field = expr` — one field of a struct local, one level deep.
            let dot = do stmtIndexOfText(target, ".")
            if dot > 0 && !contains(target, "["):
                let noExprF = do emptyNode()
                let fo = do stmtIsIdent(trim(slice(target, 0, dot)))
                let ff = do stmtIsIdent(trim(slice(target, dot + 1, len(target))))
                if !fo || !ff:
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExprF, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExprF}
                return Stmt{kind: "fieldAssign", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: e2, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExprF}
            let isIndexed = do stmtHasSuffix(target, "]")
            if isIndexed:
                let lb = do firstBracketIndex(target)
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                if lb < 0:
                    return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
                let idxText = slice(target, lb + 1, len(target) - 1)
                let ik = do tokenKinds(idxText)
                let it = do tokenTexts(idxText)
                let idxNode = do orNodes(ik, it, 0)
                return Stmt{kind: "indexAssign", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: e2, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: idxNode}
            let noExpr = do emptyNode()
            return Stmt{kind: "assign", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: e2, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}
        let noExpr = do emptyNode()
        return Stmt{kind: "unsupported", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: noExpr, args: [], body: [], elseBody: [], index: noExpr}

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 6: stmtBlockStmts — one BLOCK, i.e. one whole sibling statement
    # list (a proc's own top-level body, or an `if`/`loop`'s own indented
    # children), recursively. Returns a plain `[Stmt]` — a real array, no
    # Block/StmtSlot indirection at all, and no arena to carry alongside it
    # either now that expr.fct hands back real ExprNode values directly.
    # This is the one place `if`'s `else:` sibling lookahead lives
    # (mirroring parseProcBody's own `children[i+1]` check exactly): while
    # walking `node`'s children in order, an `if` line peeks at its
    # immediate next sibling, and if that sibling's own text is exactly
    # `else:`/`else`, consumes it as the else-branch (recursing into ITS
    # OWN children) and skips it as a statement of its own — an `else:`
    # line that is NOT immediately preceded by an `if` (e.g. the block's
    # own first statement) falls through to the `t == "else:" || t ==
    # "else"` guard just below and is silently dropped, the same
    # honest-but-inert degrade this file uses elsewhere for a shape it
    # cannot represent (parseProcBody's own real behavior here is a hard
    # parse error, "`else` with no matching `if`" — this port has no error
    # channel, so it degrades rather than crashes).
    # ============================================================
    proc stmtBlockStmts(texts: [text], parents: [int], node: int) -> [Stmt]:
        let kids = do stmtChildrenOf(node, parents)
        let m = len(kids)
        let mut stmts = []
        let mut i = 0
        loop i < m:
            let lineIdx = kids[i]
            let t = trim(texts[lineIdx])
            let isIf = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "if ")
            if isIf:
                let condRaw = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "if ")
                let condTrim = trim(condRaw)
                let condText = do stmtTrimSuffix(condTrim, ":")
                let ck = do tokenKinds(condText)
                let ct = do tokenTexts(condText)
                let cond = do orNodes(ck, ct, 0)
                let thenStmts = do stmtBlockStmts(texts, parents, lineIdx)
                let mut elseStmts = []
                let mut consumedElse = false
                if i + 1 < m:
                    let nextLineIdx = kids[i + 1]
                    let nt = trim(texts[nextLineIdx])
                    if nt == "else:" || nt == "else":
                        let es = do stmtBlockStmts(texts, parents, nextLineIdx)
                        elseStmts = es
                        consumedElse = true
                let noExpr = do emptyNode()
                let ifStmt = Stmt{kind: "if", lineIdx: lineIdx, expr: cond, args: [], body: thenStmts, elseBody: elseStmts, index: noExpr}
                stmts = append(stmts, ifStmt)
                if consumedElse:
                    i = i + 2
                else:
                    i = i + 1
            else:
                if t == "else:" || t == "else":
                    i = i + 1
                else:
                    let s = do singleStmt(texts, parents, lineIdx)
                    stmts = append(stmts, s)
                    i = i + 1
        return stmts

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 7: the end-to-end driver — raw multi-line proc-body TEXT in
    # (exactly what a proc's own indented body would look like, dedented
    # to start at column 0), a plain top-level `[Stmt]` out (no shared
    # arena left to hand back alongside it). This is
    # parseProcBody(n.Children, ...)'s own job, minus the (a) declaration-
    # header/type-checking concerns parseProc itself handles separately
    # (out of scope — this file is the BODY only) and (b) error return
    # (this port's established honest-sentinel convention instead — see
    # kind "unsupported", above).
    # ============================================================
    proc parseProcBodySrc(src: text) -> [Stmt]:
        let indents = do bodyIndents(src)
        let parents = do bodyParents(indents)
        let texts = do bodyTexts(src)
        let r = do stmtBlockStmts(texts, parents, -1)
        return r

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 8: describeStmt/describeStmts — proof the [Stmt] tree is
    # actually walkable, not just buildable (this task's own verification
    # ask), and the direct replacement for the old arena version's whole
    # STAGE 8 reader family (stmtKindAt/blockStmtCount/blockStmtRootAt/
    # ifCondIdx/ifThenBlockIdx/... — every one of those collapses into an
    # ordinary field read now: `stmt.kind`, `len(stmt.body)`,
    # `stmt.body[i]`, `stmt.expr`, `stmt.body`/`stmt.elseBody` directly).
    # This walks the real struct tree and renders a small, real (not just
    # "would work") text summary — a kind name per statement, `if(...)`/
    # `loop(...)` recursing into their own real `body: [Stmt]`/
    # `elseBody: [Stmt]` fields, exactly the "reads like what it is"
    # improvement predicate.fct's own rewrite note (Node vs. arena) already
    # demonstrated one level down.
    # ============================================================
    proc describeStmt(s: Stmt) -> text:
        if s.kind == "if":
            let thenPart = do describeStmts(s.body)
            let elsePart = do describeStmts(s.elseBody)
            return "if(" + thenPart + ";else:" + elsePart + ")"
        if s.kind == "loop":
            let bodyPart = do describeStmts(s.body)
            return "loop(" + bodyPart + ")"
        return s.kind

    proc describeStmts(stmts: [Stmt]) -> text:
        let n = len(stmts)
        let mut out = ""
        let mut i = 0
        loop i < n:
            let d = do describeStmt(stmts[i])
            if i > 0:
                out = out + ","
            out = out + d
            i = i + 1
        return out

    proc describeProcBody(src: text) -> text:
        let r = do parseProcBodySrc(src)
        let d = do describeStmts(r)
        return d

    proc topLevelStmtCount(src: text) -> int:
        let r = do parseProcBodySrc(src)
        return len(r)

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 9: name recovery — the actual variable/proc-name TEXT for a
    # naming statement kind, resolved on demand from `texts[lineIdx]`
    # (never stored on the Stmt struct itself — see Stmt's own doc for
    # why). Each mirrors the exact prefix-stripping singleStmt itself
    # already does when it first classified that same line. Unchanged by
    # this rewrite.
    # ============================================================
    proc letOrAssignName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let hasLet = do stmtHasPrefix(t, "let ")
        if hasLet:
            let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
            let rest0Trim = trim(rest0)
            let isMut = do stmtHasPrefix(rest0Trim, "mut ")
            let mut rest = rest0Trim
            if isMut:
                let stripped = do stmtTrimPrefix(rest0Trim, "mut ")
                rest = trim(stripped)
            let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
            if eq < 0:
                return ""
            return trim(slice(rest, 0, eq))
        let eq2 = do firstEqIndex(t)
        if eq2 < 0:
            return ""
        return trim(slice(t, 0, eq2))

    proc bareDoProcName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "do ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let openParen = do firstParenIndex(rest)
        if openParen < 0:
            return rest
        return trim(slice(rest, 0, openParen))

    proc letDoBoundName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let n = do letOrAssignName(lineText)
        return n

    # assignDoParts: `name = do Proc(args)` — the local and the proc.
    proc assignDoTarget(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(t)
        return trim(slice(t, 0, eq))

    proc assignDoProcName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(t)
        let rhs = trim(slice(t, eq + 1, len(t)))
        let afterDo0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "do ")
        let afterDo = trim(afterDo0)
        let openParen = do firstParenIndex(afterDo)
        if openParen < 0:
            return afterDo
        return trim(slice(afterDo, 0, openParen))

    # fieldAssignParts: `local.field = expr` — "local" / "field".
    proc fieldAssignLocal(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(t)
        let target = trim(slice(t, 0, eq))
        let dot = do stmtIndexOfText(target, ".")
        return trim(slice(target, 0, dot))

    proc fieldAssignField(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(t)
        let target = trim(slice(t, 0, eq))
        let dot = do stmtIndexOfText(target, ".")
        return trim(slice(target, dot + 1, len(target)))

    proc stmtIndexOfText(s: text, sub: text) -> int:
        let n = len(s)
        let m = len(sub)
        let mut i = 0
        loop i + m <= n:
            if slice(s, i, i + m) == sub:
                return i
            i = i + 1
        return -1

    proc letDoProcName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        let rhs0 = slice(rest, eq + 1, len(rest))
        let rhs = trim(rhs0)
        let afterDo0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "do ")
        let afterDo = trim(afterDo0)
        let openParen = do firstParenIndex(afterDo)
        if openParen < 0:
            return afterDo
        return trim(slice(afterDo, 0, openParen))

    # spawnProcName: the spawned proc's own name, for a "spawn" Stmt — `let
    # h = spawn ProcName(args)` is always bound (real grammar has no bare
    # spawn), so this only ever needs to strip `let `/`mut `/the `=`/
    # `spawn ` the same way letDoProcName strips `do `.
    proc spawnProcName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        let rhs0 = slice(rest, eq + 1, len(rest))
        let rhs = trim(rhs0)
        let afterSpawn0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "spawn ")
        let afterSpawn = trim(afterSpawn0)
        let openParen = do firstParenIndex(afterSpawn)
        if openParen < 0:
            return afterSpawn
        return trim(slice(afterSpawn, 0, openParen))

    # spawnHandleName: the handle a "spawn" Stmt binds (the `h` in `let h =
    # spawn ProcName(args)`) — identical shape to letDoBoundName, restated
    # under its own name for symmetry with spawnProcName above (both name-
    # recovery halves of the same statement should read as a pair).
    proc spawnHandleName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        return trim(slice(rest, 0, eq))

    # joinHandleName: the joined task handle's own name — for a bare "join"
    # Stmt (`join h`), just the trimmed remainder after the `join ` prefix
    # (a join takes a bare handle name, never a call, so there is no `(`
    # to isolate against, unlike do/spawn/read/write).
    proc joinHandleName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "join ")
        return trim(rest0)

    # letJoinBoundName / letJoinHandleName: the two name-recovery halves of
    # a bound "letJoin" Stmt (`let r = join h`) — the local `r` the result
    # lands in, and the handle `h` being joined.
    proc letJoinBoundName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        return trim(slice(rest, 0, eq))

    proc letJoinHandleName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        let rhs0 = slice(rest, eq + 1, len(rest))
        let rhs = trim(rhs0)
        let afterJoin0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, "join ")
        return trim(afterJoin0)

    # bareFileOpName: the declared `file` resource's own name for a bare
    # "write"/"read" Stmt (`write Name(content)` / `read Name()`) — op is
    # "write " or "read " (the caller's own prefix, WITH its trailing
    # space, so this stays one shared helper instead of two near-identical
    # ones), mirroring bareDoProcName's own prefix-then-paren isolation.
    proc bareFileOpName(lineText: text, op: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, op)
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let openParen = do firstParenIndex(rest)
        if openParen < 0:
            return rest
        return trim(slice(rest, 0, openParen))

    # letFileOpBoundName / letFileOpFileName: the two name-recovery halves
    # of a bound "letWrite"/"letRead" Stmt (`let ok = write Name(content)`
    # / `let x = read Name()`) — the local the result lands in, and the
    # declared file resource's own name. op is again "write " or "read ".
    proc letFileOpBoundName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        return trim(slice(rest, 0, eq))

    proc letFileOpFileName(lineText: text, op: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let rest0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(t, "let ")
        let rest = trim(rest0)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(rest)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        let rhs0 = slice(rest, eq + 1, len(rest))
        let rhs = trim(rhs0)
        let afterOp0 = do stmtTrimPrefix(rhs, op)
        let afterOp = trim(afterOp0)
        let openParen = do firstParenIndex(afterOp)
        if openParen < 0:
            return afterOp
        return trim(slice(afterOp, 0, openParen))

    # indexAssignTargetName: the array/map local's own name for an
    # "indexAssign" Stmt (`arr[i] = expr` / `map[k] = expr`) — the text
    # before the `[`, isolated the same two-step way letOrAssignName finds
    # a plain assign's own target (find the `=` first to isolate the whole
    # target, then find the `[` inside THAT to isolate the name from its
    # own index).
    proc indexAssignTargetName(lineText: text) -> text:
        let t = trim(lineText)
        let eq = do firstEqIndex(t)
        if eq < 0:
            return ""
        let target = trim(slice(t, 0, eq))
        let lb = do firstBracketIndex(target)
        if lb < 0:
            return ""
        return trim(slice(target, 0, lb))

    # ============================================================
    # STAGE 10: the live HTTP-exercisable driver — POST a real multi-line
    # proc-body `src` (see this file's own verification section for the
    # three hand-traced fixtures) and get back a text summary of the real
    # [Stmt] tree plus its top-level statement count. A struct value itself
    # (Stmt, or expr.fct's own ExprNode) can never be a `state` cell — it is
    # a proc-local value kind, the same restriction that already bars a map
    # literal from state/entity/wire use (see ast.Struct's own doc) — so
    # the action below calls proc-land summary functions
    # (describeProcBody/topLevelStmtCount) that do the struct-walking
    # INSIDE a proc and hand back only plain `text`/`int`, exactly the
    # same "do the struct work in a proc, expose only scalars to state"
    # shape predicate.fct's own demo actions already use for their bool
    # results.
    # ============================================================
    state stmtSummaryResult: text = ""
    state stmtCountResult: int = 0

    action runParseProcBody(src: text):
        let d = do describeProcBody(src)
        let c = do topLevelStmtCount(src)
        stmtSummaryResult = d
        stmtCountResult = c

    private view Home at "/stmt":
        box:
            text "{stmtCountResult} {stmtSummaryResult}"
